# hisab — higher mathematics library (CLI entry)
# حساب (Arabic: calculation, reckoning)
#
# This is the package CLI smoke test — it only proves the toolchain
# can build hisab, prints the version, and exits. The 35 math
# modules live in src/ (the library source) and ship as the
# dist/hisab.cyr bundle; library coverage lives in tests/*.tcyr,
# each suite including the src/ modules it exercises directly. The
# CLI stays minimal on purpose — it does not include the library.

include "lib/syscalls.cyr"
include "lib/io.cyr"

# ⭐ 2.19.0 — THE VERSION IS NO LONGER A SECOND COPY. This line read
# `println("hisab 2.18.0")`: a hand-edited literal, and the one version site
# `${file:VERSION}` cannot reach from the manifest. It is the site the
# 2.9.1 -> 2.9.2 bump silently missed, leaving the CLI reporting 2.9.1, which is
# why `scripts/version-bump.sh` grew a `sed` for it and CI grew a grep.
# `CYRIUS_PKG_VERSION` (cyrius 6.5.21) is declared by `cyrius build` from
# `[package].version`, i.e. from `${file:VERSION}` — so the copy is gone rather
# than automated, and drift here is now impossible by construction.
# ⚠ RE-PROBED ON 6.6.2 RATHER THAN TAKEN FROM THE CHANGELOG, because a constant
# derived from a dependency is a measurement and goes stale silently. It resolves
# from the ENTRY file and from an INCLUDED file (the included case was the one
# upstream fixed in 6.5.34), and a negative control confirms the VALUE TRACKS:
# with VERSION set to `9.9.9-probe+z` the built binary printed exactly that.
# ⛔ MUST NEVER APPEAR IN A `[lib]` MODULE. The symbol resolves from the
# manifest of whatever package is BUILDING, so a consumer compiling
# `dist/hisab.cyr` would get THEIR version, not hisab's. Safe here because
# `src/main.cyr` is `[build] src` and is not in `[lib]` — verified, the string
# occurs 0 times in `dist/hisab.cyr`.
# [measured: entry+included probes and a VERSION negative control on cyrius 6.6.2, 2026-09-10]
fn main() {
    print("hisab ", 6);
    println(CYRIUS_PKG_VERSION);
    return 0;
}

var r = main();
# sys_exit_group, not syscall(SYS_EXIT, ...) — SYS_EXIT is exit(2) and ends only
# the calling thread. hisab is single-threaded so either terminates, but the
# group form is the correct epilogue and is what cyrius >= 6.5.6 ships.
sys_exit_group(r);
